Yes — that sensation maps reasonably well onto real neuromuscular changes, even though your muscles do not literally have discrete gears.

As you ask for more speed, your nervous system changes several things together: it recruits more motor units, increases their firing rate, brings higher-threshold fast-twitch units into play, changes how strongly different muscle groups co-contract, increases leg stiffness and elastic recoil, and alters stride frequency, ground-contact time, posture, and arm drive. Those coordinated changes can feel surprisingly stepwise rather than smooth.

So what you may be sensing is something like:

\text{easy run}
\rightarrow
\text{fast run}
\rightarrow
\text{drive}
\rightarrow
\text{ballistic sprint}

Each regime uses a somewhat different coordination strategy. The interesting part isn’t merely “more muscle”; it’s different organization of the same machinery.

At very high speed, for example, hamstrings, glutes, calves, hip flexors, trunk, and arms stop feeling like isolated muscles and begin functioning as a tightly timed spring-and-lever system:

\text{neural command}
\rightarrow
\text{recruitment}
\rightarrow
\text{stiffness/timing}
\rightarrow
\text{force impulse}
\rightarrow
\text{flight}

The transition can genuinely feel like a transmission catching another gear.

A Pantheon way to describe it would be motor regime switching: the output request crosses a threshold and the body reorganizes its control policy rather than simply turning the existing one up louder.

And if you can reliably notice where those transitions occur—what changes in posture, tension, breathing, stride, or muscle recruitment—that’s actually useful proprioceptive information.